     * Drive the guard: revert at the timeout, notice a lost connection, ask
     * the hub how a lost one ended once the connection is back. Call it
     * from the client's poll, off the UI thread (it may write). Returns the
     * state; at once, unchanged, while another guarded call runs.
     */
    func applyPoll()  -> GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Put the settings from before back now. Returns the state:
     * [`GuardPhase::Reverted`], [`GuardPhase::RevertFailed`], or
     * [`GuardPhase::Lost`] when the connection went. After a lost USB
     * connection, it recalls the captured snapshot once the same device is
     * open again (the user's word, never queued).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when nothing is waiting and no
     * lost apply can be reverted on the open device.
     */
    func applyRevert() throws  -> GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * The guard's state, for the countdown bar: no I/O, never blocks on a
     * guarded call.
     */
    func applyStatus()  -> GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Apply `profile` to wire channels `left` and `right` as
     * [`DspiCore::autoeq_preview`] shows it. First the device's state is
     * captured as a snapshot, returned as a document and filed in the
     * snapshot store when a folder is named ([`DspiCore::snapshot_set_dir`]);
     * then every write is one undo step labelled "AutoEQ: <name>": the
     * level when applied (`apply_level` as for the preview: inputs' preamp
     * by default, outputs' gain only when asked), any crossover band the
     * limits profile asks for, then every PEQ band of both channels.
     * Values past the limits profile are held to it and reported
     * ([`AutoEqPreview::held`]); nothing outside the pair changes.
     *
     * Errors: those of [`DspiCore::autoeq_preview`], then
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], all before anything is sent; those of the
     * snapshot capture, before any write; then the errors of every setter
     * ([`DspiError::LimitExceeded`] for a high-pass the limits profile
     * cannot place, [`DspiError::Transfer`] and the rest). A failure part
     * way leaves the writes made so far as the one undo step, and the
     * snapshot filed (with a folder named).
     */
    func autoeqApply(profile: AutoEqProfile, left: UInt8, right: UInt8, applyLevel: Bool?) throws  -> AutoEqApply
    
    /**
     * What applying `profile` to wire channels `left` and `right` (two
     * inputs or two outputs) would do: each channel's PEQ bank, level,
     * crossover and response curve, what the limits profile would hold, and
     * the lines to show. Reads the mirror only: nothing is sent.
     *
     * `apply_level`: whether the profile's preamp is written; `None` is the
     * default: yes on inputs (their preamp), no on outputs (their
     * calibrated gain is kept and the preamp only suggested,
     * [`AutoEqPreview::suggested_trim_db`]). `Some(true)` on outputs writes
     * it as their gain.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`]
     * for a channel out of range, the same channel twice, an input with an
     * output, or a profile value no setter would take (a non-finite preamp,
     * a known filter with a frequency or Q not above 0).
     */
    func autoeqPreview(profile: AutoEqProfile, left: UInt8, right: UInt8, applyLevel: Bool?) throws  -> AutoEqPreview
    
    /**
     * Begin an undo group: everything written until the matching
     * [`DspiCore::end_group`] that undo can reverse is one undo step,
     * carrying `label` ([`HistoryEntry::label`]), as a drag is. Use it for
     * one action that writes several values (a linked pair, a Clear All, a
     * routing preset). Groups nest: only the outermost begin starts the
     * step and takes its label, and only the matching end ends it. The
     * group belongs to the connection: a disconnect, another device
     * opened, a re-attach, a re-read ([`DspiCore::fetch_all`]) and a change
     * that ends the history (a preset load, a bulk image, a factory reset)
     * end it at any depth, and an `end_group` after that does nothing.
     * With no device the call is ignored. An empty group records nothing.
     * A write undo cannot reverse stands apart, as in a drag. The group is
     * the connection's, not the thread's: call it on the thread that sends
     * the group's writes, in order with them.
     *
     * A group begun while a drag is marked ([`DspiCore::set_dragging`])
     * joins the drag's step instead of ending it (the step takes the
     * group's label if it had none), and its `end_group` leaves that step
     * open until the drag ends: one drag is one step.
     */
    func beginGroup(label: String) 
    
